1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,588
More than 10,000 companies now have climate targets

2
00:00:04,588 --> 00:00:07,714
validated by the Science Based Targets initiative.

3
00:00:08,164 --> 00:00:11,448
And on 11 June 2026, the rulebook behind their

4
00:00:11,448 --> 00:00:14,589
net-zero claims got its biggest rewrite yet.

5
00:00:15,039 --> 00:00:19,786
This is the Corporate Net-Zero Standard, Version 2.0. In the next few minutes:

6
00:00:19,786 --> 00:00:23,681
what it is, what's changed, and what it means for your business.

7
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:29,431
First, a quick refresher. SBTi is the world's leading body for

8
00:00:29,431 --> 00:00:33,415
validating corporate climate targets against climate science.

9
00:00:33,865 --> 00:00:38,375
In 2021, it launched the Corporate Net-Zero Standard: the first framework

10
00:00:38,375 --> 00:00:42,514
to define what a science-based net-zero target actually looks like.

11
00:00:42,964 --> 00:00:48,266
Version 1 focused on setting ambitious targets: cut emissions deeply,

12
00:00:48,266 --> 00:00:53,568
in line with 1.5°C, and neutralize what's left by 2050 at the latest.

13
00:00:55,268 --> 00:00:58,816
Version 2.0 is the product of a two-year revision.

14
00:00:59,266 --> 00:01:03,529
A first draft went to public consultation in March 2025,

15
00:01:03,529 --> 00:01:05,813
and a second in November 2025.

16
00:01:06,263 --> 00:01:10,840
After pilot testing and expert working groups, it was approved by SBTi's

17
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:15,099
independent Technical Council and adopted by its Board of Trustees.

18
00:01:15,548 --> 00:01:21,373
It was published on 11 June 2026. Validations open in early 2027,

19
00:01:21,373 --> 00:01:28,274
and from 1 February 2028 it becomes mandatory for all new target submissions.

20
00:01:29,974 --> 00:01:35,279
The biggest idea in Version 2.0 is a shift from ambition to implementation.

21
00:01:35,729 --> 00:01:40,601
As SBTi CEO David Kennedy put it: "companies have told us that they

22
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:45,399
need a partner that can help foster implementation and that's what

23
00:01:45,399 --> 00:01:49,980
the Corporate Net-Zero Standard Version 2.0 is designed to do."

24
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:55,217
So targets now run in five-year cycles: set a near-term target, implement it,

25
00:01:55,217 --> 00:01:59,506
report progress every year, and get assessed at the end of the cycle.

26
00:01:59,956 --> 00:02:04,557
Targets are pursued on a best-efforts basis. Companies that are genuinely acting,

27
00:02:04,557 --> 00:02:08,477
and transparent about the barriers they face, can stay in the system.

28
00:02:10,178 --> 00:02:13,808
Next, not every company is treated the same any more.

29
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:17,095
Version 2.0 sorts companies into two categories.

30
00:02:17,545 --> 00:02:20,185
Category A covers large companies everywhere,

31
00:02:20,185 --> 00:02:23,235
and medium-sized companies in high-income countries.

32
00:02:23,685 --> 00:02:27,756
In any country, you're Category A with net turnover of at

33
00:02:27,756 --> 00:02:31,898
least €450 million, or at least 1,000 full-time employees.

34
00:02:32,348 --> 00:02:37,349
In high-income countries, you're also Category A if your scope 1 and

35
00:02:37,349 --> 00:02:41,983
2 emissions reach 10,000 tCO2e, or you meet two of three tests:

36
00:02:41,983 --> 00:02:46,984
a €25 million balance sheet, €50 million turnover, or 250 employees.

37
00:02:47,434 --> 00:02:51,380
Everyone else is Category B, with a simpler set of requirements.

38
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,758
For them, scope 3 targets, transition plans and assurance are optional.

39
00:02:57,459 --> 00:03:01,467
Now, the target architecture, and a change that surprised many people.

40
00:03:01,917 --> 00:03:06,707
Near-term, five-year targets are the core. Scope 1 and scope 2 targets are

41
00:03:06,707 --> 00:03:11,368
required for every company. Scope 3 targets are required for Category A.

42
00:03:11,818 --> 00:03:15,516
Long-term targets are optional for scopes 2 and 3. For scope 1,

43
00:03:15,516 --> 00:03:18,568
they depend on the target-setting method you choose.

44
00:03:19,019 --> 00:03:22,698
And a full net-zero target? It's now optional for all companies.

45
00:03:22,698 --> 00:03:26,836
The standard puts its weight on what you deliver in the next five years.

46
00:03:28,535 --> 00:03:32,245
Let's go scope by scope. Scope 1, your direct emissions,

47
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:36,683
now gets its own separate target, covering 100% of those emissions.

48
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:40,882
Companies choose from a set of methods, including absolute

49
00:03:40,882 --> 00:03:44,502
linear reduction and sector-specific intensity pathways,

50
00:03:44,502 --> 00:03:48,122
consistent with reaching net zero by 2050 at the latest.

51
00:03:49,822 --> 00:03:52,406
Scope 2 (purchased electricity, heat,

52
00:03:52,406 --> 00:03:56,526
steam and cooling) also gets its own target, covering 100%.

53
00:03:56,976 --> 00:04:02,693
You can set an emissions reduction target, now based only on the location-based inventory,

54
00:04:02,693 --> 00:04:06,759
a target to raise your share of low-carbon electricity, or both.

55
00:04:06,759 --> 00:04:12,349
Low-carbon includes renewables, nuclear, and generation with carbon capture and storage.

56
00:04:12,799 --> 00:04:16,644
Buying certificates gets stricter: purchases must be matched to

57
00:04:16,644 --> 00:04:19,939
consumption within 12 months, in the same grid region,

58
00:04:19,939 --> 00:04:24,089
from generators commissioned or re-powered within the last 15 years.

59
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:28,781
Hourly matching isn't required to meet targets. But Category A

60
00:04:28,781 --> 00:04:33,297
companies using 10 GWh or more must report how much of their power

61
00:04:33,297 --> 00:04:37,538
is matched hour by hour, and early adopters can be recognized.

62
00:04:39,238 --> 00:04:43,112
Scope 3, the value chain, is where most companies' emissions sit,

63
00:04:43,112 --> 00:04:45,317
and where the rules changed the most.

64
00:04:45,767 --> 00:04:49,678
The old fixed coverage thresholds, 67% of scope 3 for

65
00:04:49,678 --> 00:04:53,368
near-term targets and 90% for long-term, are gone.

66
00:04:53,817 --> 00:04:58,219
Instead there's a significance test: your near-term target must cover

67
00:04:58,219 --> 00:05:02,940
every scope 3 category that makes up 5% or more of your scope 3 emissions.

68
00:05:03,391 --> 00:05:08,527
You can set an overarching absolute reduction target, an overarching supplier or

69
00:05:08,527 --> 00:05:13,471
customer alignment target, or targets for specific categories and activities.

70
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:16,689
Long-term scope 3 targets are optional,

71
00:05:16,689 --> 00:05:20,240
but if you set one, it must cover 100% of scope 3.

72
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:24,447
So how do you deliver these targets?

73
00:05:24,447 --> 00:05:27,998
Version 2.0 introduces an implementation hierarchy.

74
00:05:28,448 --> 00:05:32,032
First: direct action, in your own operations and value chain.

75
00:05:32,483 --> 00:05:36,066
Where that isn't feasible, you can act within shared systems

76
00:05:36,066 --> 00:05:40,426
such as electricity grids, gas grids, supply sheds or logistics networks,

77
00:05:40,426 --> 00:05:43,352
using tools like certificates and book-and-claim.

78
00:05:43,801 --> 00:05:47,751
What doesn't count? Carbon credits. Progress against your scope 1,

79
00:05:47,751 --> 00:05:50,743
2 and 3 targets is measured on your own inventory.

80
00:05:52,444 --> 00:05:54,649
So where do credits and removals fit?

81
00:05:54,649 --> 00:05:57,986
Through a new concept: Ongoing Emissions Responsibility.

82
00:05:58,436 --> 00:06:02,924
Every tonne you still emit on the way to net zero has an impact.

83
00:06:02,924 --> 00:06:07,272
Companies can take responsibility for anywhere from 1% to 100%

84
00:06:07,272 --> 00:06:11,550
of those ongoing emissions, and be recognized by SBTi for it.

85
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,776
Until 2035 this is voluntary, and it is kept separate from your

86
00:06:15,776 --> 00:06:19,373
target progress: it cannot be netted against your inventory.

87
00:06:19,823 --> 00:06:25,101
But from 2035, Category A companies must support eligible carbon removals

88
00:06:25,101 --> 00:06:29,801
equal to at least 1% of their ongoing scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions.

89
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:32,122
And at the net-zero target year,

90
00:06:32,122 --> 00:06:35,984
all residual emissions must be neutralized with eligible removals.

91
00:06:37,684 --> 00:06:41,306
Version 2.0 also raises the bar on accountability,

92
00:06:41,306 --> 00:06:43,914
especially for Category A companies.

93
00:06:44,364 --> 00:06:48,322
Your greenhouse gas inventory and target metrics need independent

94
00:06:48,322 --> 00:06:51,793
third-party assurance, at a minimum of limited assurance.

95
00:06:52,242 --> 00:06:56,089
You must publish a transition plan within 15 months of validation,

96
00:06:56,089 --> 00:07:00,810
approved by your highest governing body, and review it at least every five years.

97
00:07:01,261 --> 00:07:04,282
And you report progress every year, including the barriers

98
00:07:04,282 --> 00:07:07,147
you face and the actions you're taking to address them.

99
00:07:08,846 --> 00:07:10,491
Let's put it side by side.

100
00:07:10,941 --> 00:07:16,059
Version 1 used an SME route, allowed combined scope 1 and 2 targets,

101
00:07:16,059 --> 00:07:22,004
fixed scope 3 coverage thresholds, and required long-term targets for net zero.

102
00:07:22,454 --> 00:07:27,064
Version 2 brings Category A and B, separate scope 1 and 2 targets,

103
00:07:27,064 --> 00:07:32,791
a 5% significance test for scope 3, optional net-zero targets, mandatory assurance

104
00:07:32,791 --> 00:07:37,959
and transition plans for Category A, and Ongoing Emissions Responsibility.

105
00:07:39,659 --> 00:07:42,541
How has it landed? Reaction from sustainability

106
00:07:42,541 --> 00:07:45,669
leaders has been broadly positive, but not uniform.

107
00:07:46,119 --> 00:07:51,956
NewClimate Institute welcomed the stronger focus on near-term targets and implementation,

108
00:07:51,956 --> 00:07:55,761
but warned that high flexibility may weaken comparability,

109
00:07:55,761 --> 00:08:00,811
and called the decision not to phase in hourly matching a missed opportunity.

110
00:08:01,261 --> 00:08:04,264
Among other commentators, a common criticism is the

111
00:08:04,264 --> 00:08:07,266
lack of progress on how carbon credits can be used.

112
00:08:08,965 --> 00:08:11,660
So what should you do now? Here's a six-step checklist.

113
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:17,804
One: confirm your category, A or B. Two: get your inventory assurance-ready.

114
00:08:18,254 --> 00:08:22,277
Three: map scope 3, and find every category above 5%. Four:

115
00:08:22,277 --> 00:08:25,755
plan scope 1 and scope 2 separately, and check your

116
00:08:25,755 --> 00:08:29,915
electricity contracts against the new location and age rules.

117
00:08:30,365 --> 00:08:34,904
Five: build a transition plan your board can approve. Six: choose your timing.

118
00:08:35,354 --> 00:08:42,267
If you're setting or renewing targets in 2026, SBTi encourages using Version 1.3.1.

119
00:08:42,267 --> 00:08:46,764
Existing validated targets stay valid for their cycle,

120
00:08:46,764 --> 00:08:53,011
and Version 2.0 becomes mandatory for new submissions from 1 February 2028.

121
00:08:54,712 --> 00:08:59,556
Version 2.0 asks less about what you promise, and more about what you deliver.

122
00:09:00,006 --> 00:09:04,425
ReGeneva helps organizations turn standards like this into action,

123
00:09:04,425 --> 00:09:08,577
from GHG accounting to target setting and transition planning.

124
00:09:08,577 --> 00:09:12,929
Subscribe for more, and share this with your sustainability team.

